  <p class="reveal">If you're doing print on demand and you want the short answer: start with Printful if you'd rather pay a little more for consistency, or Printify if you want the widest catalogue and are willing to order samples from two or three providers before you commit. Either way, order the sample. "Fast shipping" claims everywhere in this table apply to locally warehoused stock only, and the only way to know what your customer will unwrap is to unwrap it yourself.</p>
  <div class="box check reveal"><span class="tag"><span class="g">✓</span> Before you trust any app</span><p>Order a sample to your own address · Check where it actually ships from for your customer's country · Read the app's rules on designs it will remove · Match your store's delivery promise to the app's real quote, not its headline.</p></div>
  <p class="deeper reveal"><span>Go deeper —</span> <a href="/learn/shopify-supplier-apps">The Supplier Apps That Plug Into Shopify, Compared →</a></p>

  <div class="chapter  reveal"><span class="bignum">07</span><div class="num">Chapter Seven</div><div class="orn"><span class="d"></span></div><h2 id="brand">Branding a store nobody knows your name behind</h2></div>
  <p class="answer reveal">A faceless store is branded with a name, a consistent look, and product photos that make the item feel real. No founder photo is needed, because customers buy from the brand and the product, not from a person.</p>
  <p class="reveal">The worry underneath "faceless" is that a store without a person feels untrustworthy. Look at what actually builds trust on a product page: clear photos, honest sizing, a delivery window that's kept, a returns page in plain English, and a brand that looks like it will still exist next month. Not one of those is a face.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>The name.</strong> Name the brand, not yourself. A good store name says who it's for or what it feels like, and it fits on a mug. Check the domain and the main social handles are free before you commit to it, and then stop agonising. Nobody chose the brand they love because of its name.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>The look.</strong> Two colours, one typeface, and the same photo style everywhere. Restraint reads as expensive. Clutter reads as a hobby. If you already have a brand from your digital business, use the same one; a store is a shelf in the same house, not a new house.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>The product photos.</strong> This is where the faceless approach has quietly become easier than it was. Your print on demand app generates mockups of your design on the blank, and AI image tools can now place that product in a styled setting: a mug on a kitchen counter in morning light, a tote on a chair in a hallway. You don't need a photographer or a model. You need one consistent style, and the sample you ordered, so you can shoot a couple of honest real-life shots on your phone next to the mockups.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>The About page.</strong> Write it in the brand's voice, about the customer. "We make things for women who work nights" says more than a paragraph about you. If you want a person on the page, the brand's persona can carry it. Your real face is never required.</p>
  <p class="reveal">One more thing, because people miss it: the brand extends into the box. A short thank-you card is often something the supplier app can include, and the packaging on most print on demand orders is plain. Put the brand name on the card and the product, and the parcel does the rest of the branding for you.</p>
  <div class="box tip reveal"><span class="tag"><span class="g">✦</span> The Edit</span><p>Customers don't trust faces. They trust consistency. Same colours, same tone, same delivery promise kept three times in a row. That's a brand, and none of it needs a photo of you.</p></div>
  <p class="deeper reveal"><span>Go deeper —</span> <a href="/learn/branding-guide">The Branding Guide →</a></p>

  <div class="chapter left reveal"><span class="bignum">08</span><div class="num">Chapter Eight</div><div class="orn"><span class="d"></span></div><h2 id="sales">The first ten sales without ads</h2></div>
  <p class="answer reveal">The first ten sales come from three places: being helpful in the groups where your customer already asks questions, a small email list of people who opted in for something useful, and one product page that answers every objection before it's raised. None of those require ads.</p>
  <p class="reveal">You built the product, the store and the fulfilment. Now the buyers, last, exactly where they belong. And the first ten are different from the next hundred. The next hundred come from search and from an email list that's grown. The first ten come from you being present, briefly and honestly, where your customer already is.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>Answer questions where they're asked.</strong> Every niche has groups, forums and comment threads where people ask "what store do you use for this," "where did you get that," "does anyone know a good one for X." Be the person who answers properly, with a real recommendation, and mention your store only when it genuinely is the answer. This isn't spamming a link. It's showing up as someone who knows the subject and happens to make the thing. Done sparingly and honestly, it brings the kind of buyer who comes back.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>Start a small email list.</strong> Not a newsletter you'll dread writing. A single useful download for your customer, connected to your store, and a handful of short emails that follow. The women who opt in are the warmest buyers you'll ever have, because they told you what they cared about before you sold them anything. A list of forty people who wanted the thing beats a following of four thousand who scrolled past it.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>Make one product page do the selling.</strong> Most first stores lose the sale on the page, not on the traffic. Read your product page as the most sceptical version of your customer. What's the fabric like? Will it shrink? How big is it really? How long until it arrives? What happens if it's wrong? Answer every one of those in plain words, on the page, with a photo where a photo helps. A page that removes doubt doesn't need more visitors; it needs the ones it already has.</p>
  <p class="reveal">Notice what isn't on this list. No ads, because you don't yet know which page converts, and ads will only tell you that expensively. No daily posting, because a feed forgets you the moment you stop feeding it. No discount codes shouted at strangers. Just a product that answers a real need, put in front of the woman who has it.</p>
  <p class="reveal">When the tenth order comes in, you'll know something no course can teach you: what she actually bought, which page she came from, and what she asked before she paid. That is the information the next hundred sales are built on.</p>
